Interview 1 (Darren – Iteration 3)

Interviewer: What was your first impression when you opened the UQ Clubs platform?
Interviewee: My first impression was that it looks clean and simple. The layout is easy to understand, so I can see popular clubs right away on the home screen. It feels like a mobile app designed for students.

Interviewer: How easy was it to find a club that matched your interests?
Interviewee: It was quite easy. Recommended clubs and categories like sports, culture, and faculty help a lot. For example, I like technology, so I quickly noticed the UQ Computing Society. The member numbers also help me see which clubs are active.

Interviewer: Did the search function and club categories help you discover relevant clubs?
Interviewee: Yes, definitely. Categories group clubs by type, so I didn’t need to scroll through all clubs. The search bar would also be useful if I had a specific club in mind, though I didn’t test it fully in the screenshots.

Interviewer: Were there any parts of the interface you found confusing or difficult to use?
Interviewee: A small issue is that UQ Board Games Club and UQ Basketball Club both use the code “UB”, which is confusing. Also, the profile page shows I have joined zero clubs, but there is no clear join button on the club detail page.

Interviewer: Did the club detail page provide enough information to decide whether to join?
Interviewee: Yes. For the Malaysian Students Association, it provides useful info like activities, location, email contact, and weekly schedule. I would feel confident joining after reading it.

Interviewer: How easy was it to navigate between different pages?
Interviewee: Very easy. The bottom navigation bar has Home, Clubs, and Profile. Each club has a “View More” button leading to details, which is clear and expected.

Interviewer: What information or features do you think are missing?
Interviewee: A join button on the detail page, upcoming event dates or a calendar, a way to contact club leaders, display of joined clubs on profile, and notifications.

Interviewer: How useful would this platform be for new UQ students?
Interviewee: Very useful. New students often don’t know where to start, and this platform centralizes all clubs, saving time compared to searching multiple websites or social media.

Interviewer: Compared to other ways of finding clubs, what do you think of this platform?
Interviewee: It’s faster and better organized than social media or asking friends. It combines structured lists and detailed information.

Interviewer: How likely would you be to use it in the future and why?
Interviewee: Very likely. It’s simple, fast, and focused on finding and learning about clubs. Adding a join button and displaying joined clubs would improve it further.
